@*
    The dashboard: productivity and cost, narrowable to one repository.

    This used to be an empty pane in the Monitoring module, where a dashboard was a
    derived view over a signal stream nobody had written yet. It is its own context
    now, and the wording has moved with it — nothing here derives from Monitoring's
    Progress Signal; every figure comes from a provider that already holds it.

    Two things this pane deliberately is not:

    It is not configurable. There is no layout to edit, nothing to add or remove,
    and no preference that outlives the surface being closed. The composition below
    is the composition, which is why a stale dashboard cannot happen here — there is
    no saved arrangement to fall out of step with the code.

    It is not a coordinator. It holds no figures, and the only thing it awaits is its
    own filter's options. Every part below starts its own fetch, carries its own
    status, and refreshes on its own, so an unconfigured Anthropic key delays and
    explains one part rather than blanking six. All this pane owns is the scope, and
    passing it down — and since a machine can only be named by reading the records
    that arrived from it, naming them is a read, and it happens here once when the
    pane opens rather than inside a property pretending to be free.

    The repositories in focus are handed in, not chosen here. The shell's header
    carries one repository scope for the whole screen — the same chips that narrow
    the task list — and this pane used to offer a second select for the same
    question, which put two repository filters on one screen answering differently.
    So the pane takes the scope whole: the repositories the chips hold, in the order
    they were taken, or none for all of them. What stays here is what only this
    surface asks: the machine, and the window.

    Two dimensions, and neither of them drives everything. The repository scope drives
    the productivity parts and visibly does not drive the cost parts or the sessions
    part — neither provider reports spend per repository, and only one of the two
    assistants records a repository against a session, so narrowing by one would drop
    Claude's half of that part rather than filter it. The machine filter drives only the
    sessions part, because GitHub cannot say which machine a pull request was worked from
    and a bill does not either. Every one of those refusals is stated on the part it
    applies to rather than left for a reader to work out from a figure that will not
    move — behind an info mark beside the part's title, so the figures are what a part
    shows and the caveat is there for whoever asks. The sections used to restate them in
    a paragraph under each heading; that was the same sentence twice on one screen, and
    it went.

    The sessions part refuses a third dimension that is not a filter at all: its activity
    grid is the last seven days whatever period is selected, and is drawn on a local clock
    while every other figure on this surface is UTC. Both are said in that part's own
    caption, because this pane owns the filters and not the meaning of what they do not
    reach.

    The sections are tabs: Overview, then one tab per section. The selected tab is a
    field like the scope: the pane opens on Overview every time and forgets the choice
    when the surface closes, for the reason nothing else here is persisted. Every panel
    renders as the pane opens and stays rendered while another tab is shown — TabPanel's
    Eager — so each part starts its fetch at once, as it did when the sections were
    folds, and switching back brings back the figures a part had, not a fresh fetch. It
    is also what lets the Devbook tab carry the open drift issues' count before anyone
    has opened that tab.

    What used to sit behind a section's More fold is shown directly in its tab: a tab
    is already the second look a fold was there to defer, so folding inside it again
    would hide half of what the reader just asked to see.

    The Overview reads across the other tabs: five headline tiles, then a main column —
    throughput, the roadmap against its pace — and a side column — Needs you, spend by
    model, hours worked. Every block on it is a part of its own, fetching through the
    insight its tab already reads, so a block shares that tab's cached read and carries
    its own loading and unavailable states rather than waiting on the others. Where the
    pane is narrow the side column wraps under the main one. A Needs-you item links to
    its tab by moving the selection here, the one place that knows the tabs.

    The "Read as brief" link at the end of the strip opens the weekly brief in place of
    the tabs, and the brief's "Open overview" brings the tabs back on the Overview. The
    tabs are not rendered while the brief is shown — the brief reads through the same
    insights, so its blocks share the tabs' cached reads over twelve weeks, and the tabs'
    parts find their figures in those caches when they come back. Like the tab, which view is shown is a
    field: the pane opens on the tabs every time. The window toggle is not offered while
    the brief is shown, because the brief reads twelve weeks whatever it says.

    The ✕ only reports that it was pressed; what that closes is the shell's business.
*@

<section @key=@("dashboard-pane") class="dashboard-panel" id="dashboard-pane" aria-labelledby="dashboard-title" data-testid="dashboard-panel">
    @* The header names the tab on show and leads with what that tab says; it is a part
       of its own (DashboardTabHeader), because the sentence comes from the tab's own
       insight and this pane holds no figures. The controls sit beside the ✕ (board
       Main): they scope the whole surface, and a row of their own under the header was
       a second header. *@
    <CascadingValue Value="_refreshes" IsFixed="true">
    <DashboardTabHeader Tab="@(_showBrief ? null : _activeTab)" Scope="_scope">
        <Actions>
            @if (ScopeNote is { } note)
            {
                @* Where the window and the machine reach nothing on the tab on show, the
                   header says so and the two controls are disabled rather than hidden:
                   moving them would change nothing there, and a control that vanished
                   would have to be found again on the next tab. *@
                <Badge Kind="area"
                       CssClass="dashboard-panel__scope-note"
                       Title="@note.Why"
                       TestId="dashboard-scope-note">@note.Text</Badge>
            }

            @* No repository control here: the header's scope chips are it — see the
               note at the top of this file. The library's SelectField rather than
               RepositorySelector, which hard-codes a repository chip. *@
            <SelectField CurrentValue="@_scope.MachineId"
                         Options="@MachineOptions"
                         Label="Machine to focus on"
                         IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                         EmptyLabel="All machines"
                         CssClass="badge badge--area"
                         Disabled="@(ScopeNote is not null)"
                         TestId="dashboard-machine-filter"
                         OnChanged="FocusMachine" />

            @if (!_showBrief)
            {
                @* One choice of four, so a radio group: one stop in the tab order, the
                   arrows move and select, and a screen reader hears one control. *@
                <ButtonGroup Radio="true" AriaLabel="How far back to look" TestId="dashboard-window-filter">
                    @foreach (var period in Periods)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton Pressed="_scope.Period == period"
                                      PressedChanged="_ => ChooseWindow(period)"
                                      Disabled="@(ScopeNote is not null)"
                                      TestId="@($"dashboard-window-{Weeks(period)}")">
                            @($"{Weeks(period)} weeks")
                        </ToggleButton>
                    }
                </ButtonGroup>
            }

            <IconButton AriaLabel="Close dashboard" CssClass="btn--ghost" OnClick="() => OnClose.InvokeAsync()">✕</IconButton>
        </Actions>
    </DashboardTabHeader>
    </CascadingValue>

    @* One refresh fan-out for the pane: a part's refresh reaches every part that reads
       the same insight, so a refresh moves every figure it could have changed. *@
    <CascadingValue Value="_refreshes" IsFixed="true">
        @if (_showBrief)
        {
            <WeeklyBrief Scope="_scope" OnOpenOverview="OpenOverview" OnOpenTab="OpenTabFromBrief" />
        }
        else
        {
            @* The library's tab strip: role="tablist", a roving tabindex, and the arrow, Home
               and End keys. Every panel is Eager — see the note at the top of this file. The
               panels keep the test ids the sections had, so a part is found where it was. *@
            <Tabs @ref="_tabs"
                  @bind-ActiveId="_activeTab"
                  AriaLabel="Dashboard sections"
                  CssClass="dashboard-tabs"
                  StripCssClass="dashboard-tabs__strip"
                  PanelCssClass="dashboard-panel__content"
                  TestId="dashboard-section-tabs"
                  ListTestId="dashboard-tablist">
                <ListTrailing>
                    @* A button rather than an anchor: it swaps the pane's view rather than
                       navigating anywhere. *@
                    <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                               CssClass="dashboard-tabs__brief"
                               OnClick="OpenBrief"
                               TestId="dashboard-brief-link">
                        Read as brief <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                    </AppButton>
                </ListTrailing>
                <ChildContent>
                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-overview" Title="Overview" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-overview" TestId="dashboard-overview">
                        @* The board's order: the tiles across the top, then the two columns. The
                           side column is an aside because it answers a second question — what
                           needs me — beside the first. *@
                        <section class="dashboard-overview__tiles" aria-label="Headline figures" data-testid="dashboard-overview-tiles">
                            <OverviewPullRequestsTile Scope="_scope" />
                            <OverviewPointsTile Scope="_scope" />
                            <OverviewAgentTimeTile Scope="_scope" />
                            <OverviewSpendTile Scope="_scope" />
                            <OverviewDriftTile Scope="_scope" />
                        </section>
                        <div class="dashboard-overview__columns" data-testid="dashboard-overview-columns">
                            <div class="dashboard-overview__main" data-testid="dashboard-overview-main">
                                <OverviewThroughputPart Scope="_scope" />
                                <OverviewRoadmapPart Scope="_scope" />
                            </div>
                            <aside class="dashboard-overview__side" aria-label="Needs you, spend and hours" data-testid="dashboard-overview-side">
                                <OverviewNeedsYouPart Scope="_scope" OnOpenTab="OpenTab" />
                                <OverviewSpendByModelPart Scope="_scope" />
                                <OverviewHoursPart Scope="_scope" />
                            </aside>
                        </div>
                    </TabPanel>

                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-productivity" Title="Productivity" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-productivity" TestId="dashboard-productivity">
                        @* The throughput row, then the volume chart with the scores beside
                           it — the chart taking the room, the scores their own width — then
                           rework across the whole tab. *@
                        <HeadlinePart Scope="_scope" />
                        <div class="dashboard-productivity__split">
                            <TrendPart Scope="_scope" />
                            <ScorePart Scope="_scope" />
                        </div>
                        <ReworkPart Scope="_scope" />
                    </TabPanel>

                    @* The backlog's side of the same question the productivity tab asks of
                       GitHub, and the roadmap's plan held against it. The repository chips
                       narrow all three; the machine filter does not, because neither a task
                       nor a plan records a machine — every part says so. The figures lead,
                       then the two weekly task charts side by side, then the roadmap's
                       timeline on the same weeks. The charts sit above it because the
                       timeline grows a row per plan and pushed them off the screen. *@
                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-tasks" Title="Tasks" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-tasks" TestId="dashboard-tasks-section">
                        <TaskFiguresPart Scope="_scope" />
                        <div class="dashboard-section__split">
                            <CompletedTasksPart Scope="_scope" />
                            <TaskEffortPart Scope="_scope" />
                        </div>
                        <RoadmapPart Scope="_scope" />
                    </TabPanel>

                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-sessions" Title="Sessions" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-sessions" TestId="dashboard-sessions-section">
                        <SessionsPart Scope="_scope" RepositoryColour="@RepositoryColour" OnOpenSessions="@OnOpenSessions" />
                        @* The person's own hours, read from the same sessions' prompts and split
                           by office hours (local ADR 0019, §7). Beside the sessions because it is
                           their record, after them because it answers a second question. *@
                        <HoursWorkedPart Scope="_scope" />
                    </TabPanel>

                    @* The devbook against the code: what the sync sweeps last said about each
                       unit, by the direction it goes, and the drift issues they left open. The
                       repository chips narrow it; the window and the machine do not, and the
                       part says so. The tab carries the open issues' count the part reports. *@
                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-devbook" Title="Devbook" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-devbook" TestId="dashboard-devbook-section">
                        <TitleContent>
                            Devbook
                            @if (_openDriftIssues is > 0)
                            {
                                <span class="dashboard-tabs__badge" aria-hidden="true" data-testid="dashboard-tab-devbook-badge">@MetricFormat.Compact(_openDriftIssues.Value)</span>
                                <span class="sr-only">, @DriftIssuesSpoken(_openDriftIssues.Value)</span>
                            }
                        </TitleContent>
                        <ChildContent>
                            <DriftPart Scope="_scope" OpenIssuesCounted="CountOpenDriftIssues" />
                        </ChildContent>
                    </TabPanel>

                    <TabPanel Id="dashboard-cost" Title="Cost" Eager="true"
                              BaseClass="dashboard-tab" TabTestId="dashboard-tab-cost" TestId="dashboard-cost">
                        <SpendThisMonthPart Scope="_scope" />
                        <SpendTrendPart Scope="_scope" />
                        <SpendByModelPart Scope="_scope" />
                    </TabPanel>
                </ChildContent>
            </Tabs>
        }
    </CascadingValue>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The four windows on offer. A closed set, because an arbitrary one
    /// would make two readings taken a fortnight apart incomparable.</summary>
    private static readonly DashboardPeriod[] Periods =
    [
        DashboardPeriod.FourWeeks,
        DashboardPeriod.EightWeeks,
        DashboardPeriod.TwelveWeeks,
        DashboardPeriod.TwentySixWeeks
    ];

    /// <summary>What the header says when the window and the machine reach nothing on
    /// the tab on show, and why — or null where they do. The Devbook tab reads the last
    /// verdict and the issues open now; the Cost tab reads the calendar month, so it
    /// names the month instead.</summary>
    private (string Text, string Why)? ScopeNote => _showBrief
        ? null
        : _activeTab switch
        {
            "dashboard-devbook" => ("Window and machine don't apply",
                "A verdict is the last one a sweep left and an issue is open now, so neither the window nor the machine narrows this tab."),
            "dashboard-cost" => (MonthName,
                "Spend is read for the calendar month, and no provider reports it per machine, so neither the window nor the machine narrows this tab."),
            _ => null
        };

    private string MonthName =>
        (Services.GetService(typeof(TimeProvider)) as TimeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System)
            .GetLocalNow().ToString("MMMM", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    [Inject]
    private IServiceProvider Services { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the dashboard is looking at. A field rather than anything persisted:
    /// closing the surface forgets it, which is the whole reason there is nothing to
    /// configure here.
    /// </summary>
    private DashboardScope _scope = DashboardScope.Default;

    /// <summary>The pane's refresh fan-out — see <see cref="DashboardRefreshes"/>.</summary>
    private readonly DashboardRefreshes _refreshes = new();

    /// <summary>The tab on show. Overview every time the pane opens; a field rather
    /// than anything persisted, for the same reason the scope is one.</summary>
    private string _activeTab = "dashboard-overview";

    /// <summary>Whether the weekly brief is shown in place of the tabs. A field, for the
    /// reason the tab is one.</summary>
    private bool _showBrief;

    /// <summary>The tab to take focus to once the tabs are back: they are rendered anew
    /// when the brief closes, so the focus waits for that render.</summary>
    private string? _focusTabOnReturn;

    /// <summary>The open devbook-drift issues the Devbook part last counted, or null
    /// while it has not, or could not - the tab shows no badge then rather than a
    /// zero that would say none are open.</summary>
    private int? _openDriftIssues;

    /// <summary>What the machine directory answered. Empty until it has, which renders
    /// as the "All machines" option on its own — the honest shape of "not asked yet",
    /// and the option the filter falls back to anyway.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<DashboardMachine> _machines = [];

    [Inject]
    private IMachineDirectory Machines { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Where the pane tells the shell what it is looking at, so the Ask
    /// AI source fetches for the same repositories and window the parts show.
    /// Written through <see cref="Scope"/> and never read back: the field above
    /// stays the pane's own, and closing the surface still forgets it.</summary>
    [Inject]
    private DashboardScopeInView ScopeInView { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The one place the scope is assigned, so the mirror cannot fall
    /// behind the field. Replaced rather than mutated, for the reason
    /// <see cref="FocusMachine"/> gives.</summary>
    private DashboardScope Scope
    {
        get => _scope;
        set
        {
            _scope = value;
            ScopeInView.Set(value);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The repositories in focus, by alias, in the order the shell's scope took
    /// them; empty for all of them. The pane never asks the directory itself, so it
    /// cannot disagree with the chips.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> RepositoryAliases { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Raised when somebody dismisses the dashboard. Whether that returns
    /// to the workspace, opens something else or does nothing is the shell's
    /// decision — the pane only reports that the ✕ was pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised by the Sessions tab's link to every session. Opening the Sessions
    /// pane is the shell's move; with nobody listening the tab draws no link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnOpenSessions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which identity hue a repository alias wears, or null while the hues are switched
    /// off — the shell's answer, handed through to the one part that draws a
    /// repository as a band. A callback rather than a lookup, for the reason the
    /// Sessions list takes the same one: which repositories exist and whether their
    /// hues are on are workspace questions, not this pane's.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<string?, int?>? RepositoryColour { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes the repositories the shell handed in into the scope. On every parameter
    /// set rather than once, because the chips can move while the dashboard is
    /// open and the parts follow the scope, not the parameter.
    /// <para>
    /// The scope is replaced rather than mutated, because every part decides whether
    /// to re-fetch by comparing the scope it last fetched for against the one it was
    /// given. A record with value equality makes that comparison correct — and it is
    /// also what makes this cheap: the same aliases produce an equal focus, and no
    /// part re-fetches for it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() =>
        Scope = _scope with { Repositories = RepositoryFocus.Of(RepositoryAliases) };

    /// <summary>
    /// The machines the filter offers, asked for once when the pane opens rather than
    /// read on every render the way the repositories are. The difference is the
    /// answer's cost: repositories are a settings store already in memory, and a
    /// machine can only be named by reading the records that arrived from it.
    /// <para>
    /// The label is the machine's name and the value is its id, which is the whole
    /// reason a machine has both: two machines can be called the same thing and one
    /// machine can be renamed, so a filter whose value was the name would merge or
    /// split what it is filtering.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> MachineOptions =>
        [.. _machines.Select(machine => new SelectorOption(machine.Id, machine.Name))];

    /// <summary>
    /// The pane's one await. Not a refresh hook: the parts each own their own, and a
    /// machine whose first record arrives while this is open turns up the next time
    /// it is opened — the same currency every figure on this surface has, because
    /// nothing here polls.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() =>
        _machines = await Machines.GetMachinesAsync();

    /// <summary>Focuses one machine, or all of them when the empty option is chosen.
    /// Replaced rather than mutated, for the reason <see cref="FocusRepository"/>
    /// gives.</summary>
    private void FocusMachine(string? id) =>
        Scope = _scope with { MachineId = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(id) ? null : id };

    private void CountOpenDriftIssues(int? count) => _openDriftIssues = count;

    private Tabs? _tabs;

    /// <summary>Shows the tab a Needs-you item links to, and takes focus to that tab:
    /// the link that was pressed is in the Overview panel, which this hides.</summary>
    private void OpenTab(string tabId)
    {
        _activeTab = tabId;
        _tabs?.FocusTab(tabId);
    }

    private void OpenBrief() => _showBrief = true;

    /// <summary>"Open overview": the tabs again, on the Overview.</summary>
    private void OpenOverview() => ReturnToTab("dashboard-overview");

    /// <summary>A brief block's link: the tabs again, on the block's tab.</summary>
    private void OpenTabFromBrief(string tabId) => ReturnToTab(tabId);

    private void ReturnToTab(string tabId)
    {
        _activeTab = tabId;
        _showBrief = false;
        _focusTabOnReturn = tabId;
    }

    protected override void OnAfterRender(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_focusTabOnReturn is not { } tabId || _tabs is null) return;

        _focusTabOnReturn = null;
        _tabs.FocusTab(tabId);
    }

    private static string DriftIssuesSpoken(int count) =>
        count == 1 ? "1 open drift issue" : $"{count} open drift issues";

    private void ChooseWindow(DashboardPeriod period) => Scope = _scope with { Period = period };

    private static int Weeks(DashboardPeriod period) => new DashboardScope(Period: period).Weeks;
}
